    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6efd0586_387a8094",
        "filename": "sha3/shake.go",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 142,
      "author": {
        "id": 11715
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-05-05T03:42:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think these should be strings. They are usually fixed and ASCII, right? (Even if they don\u0027t have to.)\n\nAlso, I would put them in the order NIST puts them, with S first and N second.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 142,
        "startChar": 18,
        "endLine": 142,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "8159c960bfa975b8fbcd03f1f5e7a65887ad0b37",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b1fa88e5_56f1046a",
        "filename": "sha3/shake.go",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 142,
      "author": {
        "id": 27471
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-05-07T19:50:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Strictly speaking N and S are defined as bit strings (see 3.1 of spec), so not necessarily character strings. In practice N \"probably\" always will be a character string (defined by NIST), but it is up to the caller to choose S and it doesn\u0027t have to be printable ASCII char. I\u0027m actually using bytes for customization string in my case (0x01, 0x02,...0xFF). Few other implementations use bytes. If you don\u0027t mind I would prefer to keep it as is, I don\u0027t think it is a problem to convert character string to bytes. \n\nAlso NIST defines the function as \"cSHAKE128(X,L, N, S)\", so I think order is OK here",
      "parentUuid": "6efd0586_387a8094",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 142,
        "startChar": 18,
        "endLine": 142,
        "endChar": 29
      },
